  "body": {
    "id": "thm:stoye-spike-reduction",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Suppose \\(\\mathcal M_x(p_x)\\) contains every Stoye spike vector \\(s_a\\). Then \\(R_x(q,p_x)=\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\\{(1-q_a)U_{ax}-\\sum_{c\\neq a}q_cL_{cx}\\}\\) for every \\(q\\in\\Delta_K\\), so \\(Q_x^*(p_x)\\) and \\(v_x(p_x)\\) equal the allocation correspondence and value in Proposition 1 of Stoye2007ManyTreatments. For \\(K=2\\), the formula reduces to the standard binary fractional minimax-regret rule.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mathcal M_x(p_x)",
      "p_x",
      "s_a",
      "L_{ax}",
      "U_{ax}",
      "R_x(q,p_x)",
      "q",
      "\\mathcal A",
      "K",
      "Q_x^*(p_x)",
      "v_x(p_x)",
      "\\Delta_K"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:joint-saddle",
      "def:rectangle",
      "lem:joint-saddle-background",
      "lem:stoye-proposition-one"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "For every \\(a\\), the definition of the marginal extrema gives \\(\\mathcal M_x(p_x)\\subseteq\\prod_c[L_{cx},U_{cx}]\\).  For \\(d=e_a-q\\), its \\(a\\)-th coefficient is \\(1-q_a\\geq0\\), while its \\(c\\)-th coefficient is \\(-q_c\\leq0\\) for \\(c\\neq a\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n h_{\\mathcal M_x(p_x)}(e_a-q)\n \\leq (1-q_a)U_{ax}-\\sum_{c\\neq a}q_cL_{cx}.\n\\]\nThe right-hand side is \\((e_a-q)^\\top s_a\\).  Under the stated spike-feasibility premise \\(s_a\\in\\mathcal M_x(p_x)\\), the reverse inequality holds by the definition of a support function.  Hence equality holds for every \\(a\\), and\n\\[\n R_x(q,p_x)=\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\\left\\{(1-q_a)U_{ax}-\\sum_{c\\neq a}q_cL_{cx}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nTaking the minimum and the complete argmin over \\(q\\in\\Delta_K\\), then applying \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}stoye\\text{-}proposition\\text{-}one}\\), proves equality with Stoye's allocation value and correspondence.\n\nWhen \\(K=2\\), write \\(q_1=1-q_0\\).  The two displayed regrets are affine functions with opposite slopes; the minimax rule is their intersection when that intersection lies in \\([0,1]\\), and otherwise the appropriate endpoint.  This is exactly the standard binary fractional minimax-regret rule, including its boundary cases.",
